	"yacc" and "lex" are two Unix utilities, along the same
lines as "make".  Nethack uses them to build some special programs
for translating the (human readable) level design files into some-
thing that Nethack can understand.

	In DJGPP's "zip file picker", one of your options is to
get things called "Bison" and "Flex".  These are DJGPP's versions
of "yacc" and "lex".  If you didn't download them, get them and
try again.

	If you already have them, and the folder they're in is on
your PATH, then the problem is probably in your makefile.  The 
makefile has to allow for several different cases:  you have "bison"
and "flex", you have versions of them which do the same thing but
are named differently, or you don't have anything, and the makefile
has to work around the problem.

	Near the top of the makefile is:
===========================================
#  Yacc/Lex ... if you got 'em.
#
# If you have yacc/lex or a work-alike set YACC_LEX to Y
#
YACC_LEX = Y
ifeq ($(YACC_LEX),Y)
DO_YACC = YACC_ACT
DO_LEX  = LEX_ACT
endif

# If YACC_LEX is Y above, set the following to values appropriate for
# your tools.
#
YACC   = bison -y
LEX    = flex
YTABC  = y_tab.c
YTABH  = y_tab.h
#If your tool produces y.tab.c and y.tab.h DOS might require
#the following instead.
#YTABC  = ytab~1.c
#YTABH  = ytab~1.h
LEXYYC = lexyy.c
=============================================

	Here are the values I used for 3.4.0.  They should work for
3.4.3, too.

YACC_LEX = Y
YACC   = bison -y
